<ArticleLayout
  title="Traceability in Practice: What Blockchain Actually Verifies"
  description="A grounded look at what blockchain-based traceability can and cannot prove about where a mineral came from."
>
  <p>
    Blockchain gets used as shorthand for trustworthy sourcing, which
    overstates what the technology itself does. A blockchain is a
    tamper-resistant ledger: once a record is entered, it is very hard
    to alter quietly. That is a genuinely useful property for a supply
    chain. It says nothing about whether the information entered into
    that ledger was accurate in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>What the technology actually secures</h2>
  <p>
    A blockchain-based traceability system creates a permanent, shared
    record of custody events: this parcel of material left this site on
    this date, was received by this aggregator, and moved on to this
    exporter. Every participant in the chain can see the same record,
    and no single participant can quietly edit history to hide a step.
    That transparency is what makes the system valuable to a buyer or
    regulator checking a chain of custody.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the real verification work happens</h2>
  <p>
    The harder, more important question is what happens at the first
    entry point: how confident can anyone be that the material logged
    as coming from a specific site actually came from there, and not
    from an unregistered or higher-risk source mixed in along the way.
    That confidence comes from site-level verification: physical
    inspection, production reconciliation against known site output, and
    relationships with the people actually running the operation.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why Orivia treats traceability as an operating discipline
    first and a technology second. Our own mining experience shapes what
    we check at that first entry point, and the blockchain layer then
    preserves and shares what has already been verified.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means for a buyer or investor</h2>
  <p>
    A responsible sourcing claim backed by blockchain is only as strong
    as the verification behind its first record. Asking who verified the
    origin, and how, matters at least as much as asking which
    technology recorded it afterward.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
